WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.420
A model never rereads your prompt.

2
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.859
It would have to thousands of times per answer.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.019
Something has to remember for it and that something has a price.

4
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.099
Step one, tokenization.

5
00:00:12.805 --> 00:00:17.145
Your sentence arrives as IDs. Seven tokens, not five words.

6
00:00:18.005 --> 00:00:21.865
Now watch how the attention network connects tokens across the canvas.

7
00:00:22.805 --> 00:00:24.105
Step two, attention.

8
00:00:24.485 --> 00:00:27.065
Every new token looks back at every old one.

9
00:00:27.769 --> 00:00:31.150
Those glances form an attention grid across the sequence.

10
00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:35.469
It calculates weighted sums of queries, keys, and values.

11
00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:40.430
The two things worth keeping, each token hands out a key and a value.

12
00:00:41.255 --> 00:00:45.595
The key is how it answers being looked up. The value is what it says when it is.

13
00:00:46.454 --> 00:00:51.195
Neither depends on anything that comes after it, which is the whole reason this works.

14
00:00:52.055 --> 00:00:55.195
Once computed, these vectors flow into the next stage.

15
00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:59.770
Step three, profill. The whole prompt goes through once in parallel.

16
00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.610
Keep the grid in memory. That is the kv cache.

17
00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:07.290
Read it again or store it in memory. Pick one.

18
00:01:08.085 --> 00:01:12.265
Step four, decode. Now each generated word costs one new row.

19
00:01:13.045 --> 00:01:16.505
Every generation step looks back at the cached keys and values.

20
00:01:17.445 --> 00:01:21.785
Step five, what it costs. You bought compute speed with GPU memory.

21
00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:25.840
And it grows linearly forever with each token generated.

22
00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.220
At 128

23
00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:28.960
k tokens,

24
00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.800
the cache for a 70 b model runs to tens of gigabytes,

25
00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:36.080
bigger than a lot of the weights it is reading from.

26
00:01:36.905 --> 00:01:39.565
Memory pressure becomes the ultimate bottleneck.

27
00:01:40.585 --> 00:01:41.485
Profill once,

28
00:01:41.785 --> 00:01:45.485
cache, then decode forever, keys, values, profill, decode.

